WEBVTT

00:00:00.802 --> 00:00:04.273 line:85%
Namaste, and welcome to Power Sector Academy.

00:00:04.965 --> 00:00:08.656 line:85%
This is the course on battery energy storage systems,

00:00:08.883 --> 00:00:13.231 line:85%
usually shortened to B E S S, or simply BESS.

00:00:13.883 --> 00:00:16.866 line:85%
Over the course we will go from the inside of a

00:00:16.886 --> 00:00:21.054 line:85%
single lithium-ion cell all the way out to a four-hundred-megawatt-hour

00:00:21.074 --> 00:00:23.785 line:85%
plant connected to the transmission grid.

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:28.115 line:85%
In this first chapter, we answer a simple question:

00:00:28.252 --> 00:00:31.448 line:85%
why do power grids need storage at all?

00:00:33.626 --> 00:00:36.789 line:85%
The basic physics of an alternating-current grid

00:00:36.809 --> 00:00:40.126 line:85%
is that generation and consumption must balance

00:00:40.146 --> 00:00:41.672 line:85%
at every instant.

00:00:42.338 --> 00:00:45.849 line:85%
Any imbalance shows up immediately as a change

00:00:45.869 --> 00:00:50.334 line:85%
in frequency. For a century this balance was kept

00:00:50.354 --> 00:00:54.006 line:85%
by adjusting the output of large spinning generators.

00:00:54.658 --> 00:00:58.001 line:85%
Today a growing share of energy comes from solar

00:00:58.021 --> 00:01:01.545 line:85%
and wind, which cannot be turned up on demand.

00:01:02.222 --> 00:01:03.838 line:85%
Storage fills the gap.

00:01:04.503 --> 00:01:08.233 line:85%
A battery can absorb energy when there is too much

00:01:08.253 --> 00:01:11.377 line:85%
and give it back when there is too little,

00:01:11.449 --> 00:01:14.728 line:85%
and it can react within a fraction of a second.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:18.336 line:85%
In this chapter we define the two numbers that

00:01:18.356 --> 00:01:22.048 line:85%
describe any storage plant, power and energy;

00:01:22.068 --> 00:01:25.424 line:85%
we list the services it can sell; we see where

00:01:25.444 --> 00:01:28.800 line:85%
storage sits in the power system; and we compare

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:32.365 line:85%
batteries with the other storage technologies.

00:01:34.578 --> 00:01:37.560 line:85%
The first thing to know about any storage plant

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:40.511 line:85%
is that it has two sizes, not one.

00:01:41.164 --> 00:01:44.920 line:85%
The power rating, in megawatts, is how fast energy

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.736 line:85%
can flow in or out.

00:01:47.401 --> 00:01:50.603 line:85%
In a battery plant it is set mostly by the power

00:01:50.623 --> 00:01:53.257 line:85%
conversion system, the inverters.

00:01:53.883 --> 00:01:56.891 line:85%
The energy capacity, in megawatt-hours,

00:01:56.912 --> 00:01:59.417 line:85%
is how much energy can be stored.

00:02:00.082 --> 00:02:03.566 line:85%
It is set mostly by the number of battery cells.

00:02:04.205 --> 00:02:07.742 line:85%
Divide energy by power and you get the duration,

00:02:07.762 --> 00:02:12.008 line:85%
in hours. On the left, a one-hundred-megawatt plant

00:02:12.028 --> 00:02:15.100 line:85%
with four hundred megawatt-hours can run at full

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:16.917 line:85%
power for four hours.

00:02:17.569 --> 00:02:20.538 line:85%
On the right, a two-hundred-megawatt plant with

00:02:20.558 --> 00:02:23.696 line:85%
the same four hundred megawatt-hours can run for

00:02:23.716 --> 00:02:28.708 line:85%
only two hours. Same energy, very different products.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.291 line:85%
When someone says, a four-hundred-megawatt-hour

00:02:32.311 --> 00:02:35.924 line:85%
battery, always ask for the power as well.

00:02:36.525 --> 00:02:39.752 line:85%
In the industry the convention is to write both:

00:02:39.811 --> 00:02:44.245 line:85%
one hundred megawatts, slash, four hundred megawatt-hours.

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:49.587 line:85%
This chart shows the problem that most grid-scale

00:02:49.607 --> 00:02:51.854 line:85%
batteries are built to solve.

00:02:52.482 --> 00:02:55.851 line:85%
The grey line is customer demand over a day.

00:02:56.516 --> 00:03:00.452 line:85%
The orange line is net load: demand minus solar

00:03:00.472 --> 00:03:04.691 line:85%
output. Net load is what the rest of the fleet,

00:03:04.737 --> 00:03:08.209 line:85%
the thermal and hydro plants, must supply.

00:03:08.848 --> 00:03:13.068 line:85%
With a lot of solar, net load collapses at midday

00:03:13.088 --> 00:03:16.573 line:85%
and then shoots up in the evening as the sun sets

00:03:16.593 --> 00:03:18.157 line:85%
and people come home.

00:03:18.784 --> 00:03:21.856 line:85%
The shape looks like a duck, hence the name.

00:03:22.482 --> 00:03:26.057 line:85%
The steep evening ramp is hard and expensive for

00:03:26.077 --> 00:03:29.421 line:85%
thermal plants to follow, and the midday dip may

00:03:29.441 --> 00:03:31.534 line:85%
force solar to be curtailed.

00:03:32.186 --> 00:03:35.026 line:85%
A battery charges during the midday surplus,

00:03:35.124 --> 00:03:38.519 line:85%
shown in green, and discharges into the evening

00:03:38.539 --> 00:03:40.606 line:85%
peak, shown in purple.

00:03:41.245 --> 00:03:44.253 line:85%
The blue dashed line is what the thermal fleet

00:03:44.273 --> 00:03:47.849 line:85%
now sees: a shallower valley and a smaller,

00:03:47.869 --> 00:03:51.998 line:85%
gentler peak. In India you can see this pattern

00:03:52.018 --> 00:03:55.980 line:85%
every day on the power exchanges: prices are lowest

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.188 line:85%
around midday, when solar is plentiful,

00:03:59.247 --> 00:04:02.577 line:85%
and highest in the evening hours after sunset.

00:04:03.242 --> 00:04:06.804 line:85%
This single picture explains energy arbitrage,

00:04:06.967 --> 00:04:10.747 line:85%
solar firming, peak capacity and ramp reduction,

00:04:10.845 --> 00:04:14.215 line:85%
which are four of the most important revenue streams

00:04:14.235 --> 00:04:15.897 line:85%
for a BESS.

00:04:18.098 --> 00:04:21.776 line:85%
A battery is unusual because it can provide services

00:04:21.796 --> 00:04:24.817 line:85%
across a huge range of time scales.

00:04:25.457 --> 00:04:27.950 line:85%
Read the chart from top to bottom.

00:04:28.602 --> 00:04:31.829 line:85%
The fastest services act within milliseconds to

00:04:31.849 --> 00:04:35.296 line:85%
a couple of seconds: fast frequency response and

00:04:35.316 --> 00:04:36.803 line:85%
synthetic inertia.

00:04:37.416 --> 00:04:41.314 line:85%
Then primary frequency control, often called frequency

00:04:41.334 --> 00:04:44.548 line:85%
containment reserve, acts within seconds.

00:04:45.187 --> 00:04:48.736 line:85%
Secondary reserve, or automatic generation control

00:04:48.756 --> 00:04:52.602 line:85%
regulation, acts over tens of seconds to minutes.

00:04:53.254 --> 00:04:56.507 line:85%
Tertiary reserve and balancing act over minutes

00:04:56.527 --> 00:05:00.528 line:85%
to an hour. Energy arbitrage and peak shifting

00:05:00.548 --> 00:05:05.591 line:85%
act over hours. Capacity, or resource adequacy,

00:05:05.664 --> 00:05:08.775 line:85%
needs several hours of sustained output at the

00:05:08.795 --> 00:05:13.195 line:85%
system peak. The shaded band shows where lithium-ion

00:05:13.215 --> 00:05:17.113 line:85%
batteries are strong: from milliseconds up to about

00:05:17.133 --> 00:05:18.761 line:85%
four to eight hours.

00:05:19.426 --> 00:05:22.641 line:85%
Beyond that, the cost of the extra cells makes

00:05:22.661 --> 00:05:26.765 line:85%
other technologies, such as pumped hydro or hydrogen,

00:05:26.785 --> 00:05:31.404 line:85%
more attractive. In practice, operators stack several

00:05:31.424 --> 00:05:34.715 line:85%
services on the same plant: for example,

00:05:34.736 --> 00:05:38.324 line:85%
frequency reserve during some hours and arbitrage

00:05:38.344 --> 00:05:42.408 line:85%
during others. This is called revenue stacking,

00:05:42.481 --> 00:05:45.097 line:85%
and we model it in chapter eight.

00:05:47.346 --> 00:05:50.766 line:85%
Batteries can be placed anywhere along the electricity

00:05:50.786 --> 00:05:54.143 line:85%
supply chain, and the location decides what the

00:05:54.163 --> 00:05:57.984 line:85%
battery is for. At the generation side,

00:05:58.082 --> 00:06:01.991 line:85%
a battery is co-located with a solar or wind plant.

00:06:02.669 --> 00:06:05.987 line:85%
It captures energy that would otherwise be clipped

00:06:06.007 --> 00:06:09.195 line:85%
or curtailed and shifts it to later hours.

00:06:09.847 --> 00:06:12.868 line:85%
On the transmission grid, a standalone battery

00:06:12.888 --> 00:06:15.355 line:85%
participates in wholesale markets:

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:19.441 line:85%
arbitrage, reserves, capacity, and relieving congested

00:06:19.461 --> 00:06:22.881 line:85%
lines. On the distribution network,

00:06:23.017 --> 00:06:26.734 line:85%
a battery at a substation or on a feeder can defer

00:06:26.754 --> 00:06:30.522 line:85%
an expensive network upgrade and support voltage.

00:06:31.239 --> 00:06:34.389 line:85%
Behind the meter at a commercial or industrial

00:06:34.409 --> 00:06:37.739 line:85%
site, a battery cuts the peak demand that sets

00:06:37.759 --> 00:06:41.334 line:85%
the customer's demand charge and provides backup.

00:06:41.973 --> 00:06:45.716 line:85%
At a home, it stores rooftop solar for the evening,

00:06:45.839 --> 00:06:48.964 line:85%
and many homes together can be aggregated into

00:06:48.984 --> 00:06:50.729 line:85%
a virtual power plant.

00:06:51.393 --> 00:06:53.924 line:85%
The red line is the customer's meter.

00:06:54.589 --> 00:06:57.456 line:85%
Everything to its left is front of the meter,

00:06:57.476 --> 00:07:01.399 line:85%
utility-scale. Everything to its right is behind

00:07:01.419 --> 00:07:04.866 line:85%
the meter. The technology is the same,

00:07:04.886 --> 00:07:08.139 line:85%
but the sizes, the standards and the economics

00:07:08.159 --> 00:07:08.997 line:85%
differ.

00:07:11.242 --> 00:07:13.656 line:85%
Let us make the services concrete.

00:07:14.336 --> 00:07:17.460 line:85%
The table lists each service, what the battery

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:21.003 line:85%
physically does, how fast it must respond,

00:07:21.063 --> 00:07:23.826 line:85%
how long it must sustain the response,

00:07:23.846 --> 00:07:26.596 line:85%
and which battery parameter matters most.

00:07:27.248 --> 00:07:28.697 line:85%
Notice the pattern.

00:07:29.323 --> 00:07:33.594 line:85%
Fast services, at the top, need power and speed

00:07:33.614 --> 00:07:35.514 line:85%
but very little energy.

00:07:36.192 --> 00:07:39.806 line:85%
A battery that sells only primary frequency response

00:07:39.826 --> 00:07:42.808 line:85%
might have one hour of storage or less.

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:46.106 line:85%
The energy services, in the middle,

00:07:46.153 --> 00:07:49.574 line:85%
need energy, a good round-trip efficiency,

00:07:49.594 --> 00:07:53.014 line:85%
and a battery that can tolerate a daily cycle without

00:07:53.034 --> 00:07:54.663 line:85%
wearing out too fast.

00:07:55.328 --> 00:07:58.684 line:85%
Capacity services need the plant to sustain its

00:07:58.704 --> 00:08:01.751 line:85%
output for several hours at the system peak;

00:08:01.771 --> 00:08:05.423 line:85%
the qualifying duration is set by the market rules,

00:08:05.483 --> 00:08:08.684 line:85%
which is why so many recent projects are four-hour

00:08:08.704 --> 00:08:13.298 line:85%
systems. At the bottom, voltage support and black

00:08:13.318 --> 00:08:16.210 line:85%
start depend mainly on the inverter,

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:17.640 line:85%
not on the cells.

00:08:18.279 --> 00:08:21.222 line:85%
Black start, energising a dead grid,

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:24.934 line:85%
requires a grid-forming inverter, which we cover

00:08:24.954 --> 00:08:26.437 line:85%
in chapter six.

00:08:28.658 --> 00:08:31.214 line:85%
This chart is called a Ragone plot,

00:08:31.377 --> 00:08:34.514 line:85%
after the engineer David Ragone who popularised

00:08:34.534 --> 00:08:37.696 line:85%
it for comparing energy storage devices.

00:08:38.337 --> 00:08:41.409 line:85%
The horizontal axis is specific energy,

00:08:41.546 --> 00:08:45.662 line:85%
in watt-hours per kilogram: how much energy a kilogram

00:08:45.682 --> 00:08:47.195 line:85%
of the device holds.

00:08:47.847 --> 00:08:50.636 line:85%
The vertical axis is specific power,

00:08:50.669 --> 00:08:54.322 line:85%
in watts per kilogram: how fast that kilogram can

00:08:54.342 --> 00:08:58.226 line:85%
deliver it. Both axes are logarithmic.

00:08:58.930 --> 00:09:02.441 line:85%
The dotted diagonals are lines of constant discharge

00:09:02.461 --> 00:09:06.267 line:85%
time, because time is energy divided by power.

00:09:06.919 --> 00:09:10.959 line:85%
Supercapacitors, top left, deliver huge power but

00:09:10.979 --> 00:09:14.760 line:85%
hold very little energy: they empty in seconds.

00:09:15.412 --> 00:09:17.995 line:85%
Nickel-metal hydride sits lower down,

00:09:18.015 --> 00:09:20.894 line:85%
with modest energy and modest power.

00:09:21.546 --> 00:09:24.116 line:85%
The lithium-ion family, on the right,

00:09:24.136 --> 00:09:27.968 line:85%
is the only group that is strong in both directions,

00:09:28.015 --> 00:09:29.876 line:85%
which is why it dominates.

00:09:30.528 --> 00:09:33.832 line:85%
Within the lithium family, nickel-manganese-cobalt

00:09:33.852 --> 00:09:37.634 line:85%
holds the most energy, iron-phosphate sits slightly

00:09:37.654 --> 00:09:41.358 line:85%
lower, and lithium titanate trades energy for very

00:09:41.378 --> 00:09:43.497 line:85%
high power and long life.

00:09:44.162 --> 00:09:47.312 line:85%
The vanadium flow battery looks poor here because

00:09:47.332 --> 00:09:50.392 line:85%
weight hardly matters for a stationary plant;

00:09:50.412 --> 00:09:54.000 line:85%
its strengths, cheap added energy and very long

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:57.350 line:85%
life, do not show on a per-kilogram chart.

00:09:58.015 --> 00:10:00.765 line:85%
Always remember that for grid storage,

00:10:00.901 --> 00:10:04.245 line:85%
cost per kilowatt-hour and life matter more than

00:10:04.265 --> 00:10:05.038 line:85%
weight.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:10.543 line:85%
Here are the main storage technologies side by

00:10:10.563 --> 00:10:14.823 line:85%
side. The numbers are typical ranges and vary by

00:10:14.843 --> 00:10:19.191 line:85%
project. Lithium-ion with iron-phosphate chemistry

00:10:19.211 --> 00:10:22.735 line:85%
is now the default choice for anything from half

00:10:22.755 --> 00:10:24.706 line:85%
an hour to about eight hours.

00:10:25.345 --> 00:10:28.469 line:85%
Its alternating-current round-trip efficiency,

00:10:28.593 --> 00:10:31.511 line:85%
meaning what you get back at the grid connection

00:10:31.531 --> 00:10:35.003 line:85%
divided by what you put in, is typically eighty-five

00:10:35.023 --> 00:10:38.636 line:85%
to ninety percent, including auxiliary consumption.

00:10:39.301 --> 00:10:43.199 line:85%
Pumped hydro is still the largest storage technology

00:10:43.219 --> 00:10:46.614 line:85%
in the world by installed energy, and it lasts

00:10:46.634 --> 00:10:50.364 line:85%
a century, but it needs suitable geography and

00:10:50.384 --> 00:10:51.910 line:85%
takes years to build.

00:10:52.575 --> 00:10:56.382 line:85%
Compressed air and hydrogen offer long durations

00:10:56.402 --> 00:10:58.211 line:85%
but lower efficiency.

00:10:58.863 --> 00:11:02.232 line:85%
Flow batteries are interesting because power and

00:11:02.252 --> 00:11:05.905 line:85%
energy are sized independently, which we will see

00:11:05.925 --> 00:11:07.258 line:85%
in chapter three.

00:11:07.897 --> 00:11:11.459 line:85%
Flywheels and supercapacitors are for power-only

00:11:11.479 --> 00:11:16.459 line:85%
services. The rest of this course focuses on lithium-ion,

00:11:16.531 --> 00:11:19.732 line:85%
because that is what you will meet in almost every

00:11:19.752 --> 00:11:23.985 line:85%
BESS project today, but we will treat the alternatives

00:11:24.005 --> 00:11:25.281 line:85%
when they matter.

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:29.582 line:85%
Let us recap chapter one.

00:11:30.236 --> 00:11:33.179 line:85%
A storage plant always has two sizes:

00:11:33.277 --> 00:11:37.019 line:85%
power in megawatts and energy in megawatt-hours,

00:11:37.079 --> 00:11:38.927 line:85%
so always quote both.

00:11:39.617 --> 00:11:42.871 line:85%
Batteries earn their keep by moving cheap midday

00:11:42.891 --> 00:11:45.769 line:85%
solar into the expensive evening peak.

00:11:46.473 --> 00:11:49.287 line:85%
One plant can stack several services,

00:11:49.398 --> 00:11:51.414 line:85%
from milliseconds to hours.

00:11:52.079 --> 00:11:55.487 line:85%
And lithium-ion with iron-phosphate chemistry is

00:11:55.507 --> 00:11:59.030 line:85%
the default choice for durations from half an hour

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:00.602 line:85%
to about eight hours.

00:12:01.254 --> 00:12:04.636 line:85%
The slides for this chapter are available to download

00:12:04.656 --> 00:12:06.401 line:85%
just below this video.

00:12:07.066 --> 00:12:10.074 line:85%
Take the short quiz to check your understanding,

00:12:10.249 --> 00:12:13.592 line:85%
and in the next chapter, we go inside the cell

00:12:13.612 --> 00:12:15.885 line:85%
to look at its electrochemistry.

00:12:16.563 --> 00:12:18.339 line:85%
Thank you for watching.
